#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197985 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83edde2a-daa5-50ce-b48c-3ded50a1a179" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"}] :question/id #uuid "985e1b18-0023-55ba-b4e4-69a109d8de58" :question/math-academy-id "e-12485" :question/problem "A plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $y$-axis and the lines $x=\\dfrac{y}2$ and $x=y-2$, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is $\\lambda(x,y) =\\dfrac y8$. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $x=2$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4173/Source/Images/11eed00af0f2d1ea5c3dd9df00a40291.png)" :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D$.\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 2x \\leq y \\leq x+2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4173/Source/Images/3888077dfe4169493752346eb2247621.png)\n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y) = \\mid x - 2 \\mid\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &= ∬_(D)λ(x, y)[r(x, y)]^{2}dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}∫_{2x}^{x + 2}(\\frac{y}{8}) \\mid x - 2 \\mid ^{2}dydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}∫_{2x}^{x + 2}(\\frac{y}{8})(x - 2)^{2}dydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(x - 2)^{2}∫_{2x}^{x + 2}\\frac{y}{8}dydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(x - 2)^{2}[\\frac{y^{2}}{16}]_{2x}^{x + 2}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{16}∫_{0}^{2}(x - 2)^{2}((x + 2)^{2} - (2x)^{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{16}∫_{0}^{2}(x^{2} - 4x + 4)(4 + 4x - 3x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{16}∫_{0}^{2}(16 - 24x^{2} + 16x^{3} - 3x^{4})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{16}[16x - 8x^{3} + 4x^{4} - \\frac{3x^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{16}(32 - 64 + 64 - \\frac{96}{5}) - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{16}(\\frac{64}{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197986 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7321a6d4-007e-5813-bfa7-9e06d0789358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"}] :question/id #uuid "eab955a5-019b-5219-b5d5-32e3818be4b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12486" :question/problem "A plate with mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the curves $y=x$ and $y=4x-x^2$, as shown above. Given that the region has an area of $\\dfrac92$ square units, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $x=3$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4173/Source/Images/b0b9a3962cb0c3c1dde403ebea8607ff.png)" :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D$.\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\:\\: x \\leq y \\leq 4x-x^2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4173/Source/Images/03b423d9e4de7171ae11eedbfe4af11d.png)\n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y) = \\mid x - 3 \\mid\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{2M}{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &= ∬_(D)λ(x, y)[r(x, y)]^{2}dA \\\\\n&= \\frac{2M}{9}∫_{0}^{3}∫_{x}^{4x - x^{2}} \\mid x - 3 \\mid ^{2}dydx \\\\\n&= \\frac{2M}{9}∫_{0}^{3}∫_{x}^{4x - x^{2}}(x - 3)^{2}dydx \\\\\n&= \\frac{2M}{9}∫_{0}^{3}(x - 3)^{2}[y]_{y = x}^{y = 4x - x^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{2M}{9}∫_{0}^{3}(x^{2} - 6x + 9)(3x - x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{2M}{9}∫_{0}^{3} - x^{4} + 9x^{3} - 27x^{2} + 27xdx \\\\\n&= \\frac{2M}{9}[- \\frac{x^{5}}{5} + \\frac{9x^{4}}{4} - 9x^{3} + \\frac{27x^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2M}{3^{2}}(-\\frac{3^{5}}{5} + \\frac{3^{6}}{4} - 3^{5} + \\frac{3^{5}}{2}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3^{3}M(-\\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{3}{4} - 1 + \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{27M}{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197988 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4820138b-483c-5ced-8df9-9849f5f2a7cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"}] :question/id #uuid "0f7dbe9f-b448-5e76-9932-68e1ff99904e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12488" :question/problem "A plate with mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant enclosed between the curve $y=\\sqrt x$ and the line $x=1$, as shown above.\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to its distance from the $x$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $x=-1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4173/Source/Images/edc9cb439dea888f2ce37f4d8ad17b5c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D$.\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt x \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4173/Source/Images/37d6c9a2f0b721457fa666b86f4e9c8d.png)\n\nAlso, the distance between a point and the line $x=-1$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y) = \\begin{vmatrix}x-(-1) & = & x+1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, the mass density can be written as\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = k \\mid y \\mid = ky\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find the value of $k$ by integrating the mass density function over the plate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= ∬_(D)λ(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}kydydx \\\\\n&= k∫_{0}^{1}[\\frac{1}{2}y^{2}]_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{2}∫_{0}^{1}(x - 0)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{2}∫_{0}^{1}xdx \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{2} \\cdot [\\frac{1}{2}x^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = k y = 4M y\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{l} &= ∬_(D)λ(x, y)[r(x, y)]^{2}dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}4My \\mid x + 1 \\mid ^{2}dydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}4My(x + 1)^{2}dydx \\\\\n&= 4M∫_{0}^{1}(x + 1)^{2}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}ydydx \\\\\n&= 4M∫_{0}^{1}(x + 1)^{2}[\\frac{1}{2}y^{2}]_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}dx \\\\\n&= 2M∫_{0}^{1}(x + 1)^{2}(x - 0)dx \\\\\n&= 2M∫_{0}^{1}(x^{3} + 2x^{2} + x)dx \\\\\n&= 2M[\\frac{1}{4}x^{4} + \\frac{2}{3}x^{3} + \\frac{1}{2}x^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 2M(\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= 2M \\cdot \\frac{17}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{17M}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7321a6d4-007e-5813-bfa7-9e06d0789358" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc42c505-27f3-5ee5-a0a5-84b00ecd61e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4M}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194352} {:answer/id #uuid "e4103500-e73e-58af-a06c-e42cbcc40130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9M}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194353} {:answer/id #uuid "11e077c3-7d19-5963-9c3b-c2737df6b0ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5M}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194354} {:answer/id #uuid "30525621-69e0-58a6-9180-b3ce74192dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5M}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194355} {:answer/id #uuid "54d6a489-a12e-5e9b-b1e5-ef14ecb97f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8M}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11e077c3-7d19-5963-9c3b-c2737df6b0ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5M}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194354} :answer-field/id #uuid "c21ac635-708a-5a08-8064-d553a446b635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194351}] :question/id #uuid "a9aa46eb-8f5c-53d3-8081-5b060771cd18" :question/math-academy-id "q-163000" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4173/Source/Images/q-163000.png)\n\nA plate with mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the curves $y = x^{2}$ and $y = \\sqrt{x}$, as shown above. Given that the region has an area of $\\frac{1}{3}$, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $x = 1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4820138b-483c-5ced-8df9-9849f5f2a7cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "931466d1-3f1f-597a-b12d-4483277eca8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194364} {:answer/id #uuid "b82712f4-df0c-5fa5-b4ea-63a77af230ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194365} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f61d79-b8d8-5a58-a045-34f877d11e3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194366} {:answer/id #uuid "4570ceff-153a-5f32-b9b8-cfabdc920373" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194367} {:answer/id #uuid "47c79474-7535-55c4-8ad2-5d3658d580a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{42M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "931466d1-3f1f-597a-b12d-4483277eca8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194364} :answer-field/id #uuid "59d4667d-aa04-5fc6-a325-94d8cf620f99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194363}] :question/id #uuid "28b15881-bdd0-5404-83c9-3db9be1582eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-163008" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4173/Source/Images/q-163008.png)\n\nA triangular plate with mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $x$-axis and the lines $x = 1$ and $y = 2x$.\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to its distance from the $x$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $x =-2$."}]}
